  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/9/9a/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_8.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "As Midtown High's science teacher wheels in a strange-looking robot device, Flash shoves Peter, causing his glasses to fall off and shatter.&nbsp; Though Peter acknowledges that he no longer needs the glasses, he's frustrated at taking the constant abuse from Flash.&nbsp; The teacher then introduces Mr. Petty of the \"I.C.M Corporation\" (<i>clearly a derivative of IBM</i>), who shows off the \"Living Brain.\"&nbsp; Mr. Petty has Peter Parker step forward to ask it a question, and the class prompts him to ask what Spider-Man's real identity is.&nbsp; Peter is terrified that it might be smart enough to guess correctly!\n\nWhile they watch, the two robot attendants consider how owning something like the Living Brain could be used to predict horse races and so forth.&nbsp; They decide to steal it.&nbsp; Peter feeds the information into the Brain, and out comes a coded piece of paper that he's given the job to translate overnight.&nbsp; Flash tries to take it from him, but Peter holds him back, and the science teacher suggests they settle the matter \"in the gym!\"\n\nAfter class, with his boxing trunks on, Peter is worried he'll hurt Flash.&nbsp; But his spider abilities make him able to move so fast that Flash can't even get close, and it looks to the watching students that he's simply staying out of Flash's reach.&nbsp; Finally, Peter uses only the tiniest fraction of his power and throws a punch, and Flash is thrown out of the ring, completely dazed.&nbsp; Thinking it was just a lucky punch, he reengages.\n\nBut while they're fighting, the two attendants make their move to steal the machine.&nbsp; They push Petty away and accidentally bump the machine, which seems to come to life!&nbsp; It begins to run wild through the school.&nbsp; Meanwhile, in the gym, Flash is realizing that no matter how hard he tries to connect, Peter seems able to dodge every punch.&nbsp; Peter decides to just flick his wrist to KO Flash, but there is commotion from outside the gym just as he throws his punch, and Flash turns to look, so it appears to everyone that it's a foul.&nbsp; Peter struck while his opponent's head was turned!\n\nPeter carries Flash to the locker room, and soon Spider-Man is on the scene to confront the robot, which is running amok through the hallways.&nbsp; Spider-Man probes and tests and begins to realize that the Brain is learning and is very likely to be able to predict his movements before long.&nbsp; He continues to evade it while occasionally having to stop to let kids out of the school.&nbsp; The Brain is learning more and more and is becoming a threat even to Spider-Man.\n\nBut the hero eventually jumps for the robot's controls, managing to finally deactivate it.\n\nThe two attendants run for cover but bump into the now recovered Flash Thompson, who is getting dressed in the locker room.&nbsp; They are accidentally knocked out.&nbsp; Everyone sees what looks like Flash capturing the two crooks, and Peter can't resist suggesting that he's Spider-Man.&nbsp; For starters, he wasn't around when the robot attacked, he tried to take the secret identity paper from him, and of course, it had to look like he lost the fight to Peter Parker so that no one would suspect who he really is!&nbsp; Peter smirks as Flash continues to try and deny it.\n\nOn his way home from school, Peter decides to claim he lost the paper during all the excitement.&nbsp; And the day wasn't a complete failure, as he got to clobber Flash without giving himself away.&nbsp; \"It's been a mighty pleasant day!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=FOR THE RECORD:]\n[BR] This is a review of the <b>first</b> of the two stories found in this magazine.\n[BR] My review on the other story is posted elsewhere.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nA largely solid effort.&nbsp; I'm sure it was a thrill for the readers of the day to finally get to see Peter and Flash go at it.&nbsp; That part, and Peter's accusing Flash of being Spider-Man was <i>well</i> done and worthy of an even higher grade than what I awarded, and the middle panel of page 6 is not to be missed (and a fantastic job by Ditko, I might add)!\n\n\n[ASSET: left | SettleItInTheGym | width=12%] [ASSET: right | FlashBrained | width=12%]\n\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: left | FlashKOed | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: center | FlashIsSpiderMan | width=67%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\nBut the Living Brain kind of spoiled it and dragged the book back down to Earth.&nbsp; That seemed like a mostly rushed-together plot.&nbsp; I'll give the creators credit enough to have used it wisely to set up the boxing match, because that's what it ultimately amounted to.&nbsp; Even <i>if</i> the Living Brain could learn and predict (a tall order projecting AI onto a 1960s goofy-looking robot), since when could Spider-Man not just grab hold of the hunk of metal and tear it to pieces?&nbsp; That was what did not make sense to me, and we get the standard handful of pages of mostly-pointless action while the two spar - <i>yawn</i>.\n\n[ASSET: right | UnvariedLayout | width=19%] Ditko's unimaginative battle sequences contrast sharply with his excellent close-ups, and the 9-panel layout is rarely varied over the entire second half of the story, including [SPAN: severity=warning; text=four straight pages] (13-16).\n[BR]\n[BR] This only helps strengthen the argument that Ditko's true strength as illustrator lies elsewhere -- the frequent down-to-earth nature of the strip in general -- and it's perhaps no surprise that the <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> magazine is noted for its supporting cast, which Ditko excels at depicting, each one having their own unique style.\n[BR]\n[BR] But he does not possess the Kirby knack of dynamic action, despite the fact that Spider-Man's odd motions <i>do</i> lend themselves to his style.&nbsp; (I merely point it out to reinforce the fact that editor Stan Lee knew what he was doing by allowing Steve Ditko to draw the strip in the first place.)\n\nIt goes without question that this story is notable more for the boxing match than with the forgettable Living Brain.\n\n\n[ASSET: left | FacialExpressions | width=12%] While Peter's joke of trying to get other students to think that Flash is Spider-Man, it should be noted that on page 12 of June's [LINK:1963:ASM_4|ASM #4|2026-10-26], it <i>clearly</i> shows Flash in the classroom with the other students, cheering for Spider-Man when he entered to challenge the Sandman.&nbsp; (I suspect he already <i>knew</i> this, but still wanted to have a little fun at the expense of the school bully.)\n\nNote that this is the second time in four issues that Spider-Man has shown up in Midtown High (see [LINK:1963:ASM_4|ASM #4|2026-10-26] for the first one).\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nBut despite the distaste I had of the Living Brain, I did enjoy the issue, and it certainly is worthy of a slightly above mid-grade rating.",
  "decoder_ring": "Note that <i>Web of Spider-Man</i> #35 (Feb 1988 cover date) pays homage to this cover and to the story to some degree in an issue that was pretty decent its own right, but of course, we'll get there <i>much </i>later!",
  "highlight": "FIXME -- write something"
}